+# Settings profiles (`forge profile`)
+
+One command flips a repo's whole operating regime. A **settings profile** is a
+named snapshot of Forge's entire settings surface — rails, gates, stage phases,
+adapters, role bindings and protected paths — applied atomically.
+
+```bash
+forge profile list                   # saved profiles, active one marked
+forge profile show                   # which regime this repo is actually in
+forge profile apply strict           # flip the whole regime
+forge profile dump                   # the fully-resolved regime, every setting
+forge profile create my-regime       # save the current regime under a name
+forge profile save [--as <name>]     # fold adjustments back into a profile
+forge profile export <name> <path>   # write a shareable bundle
+forge profile import <path>          # install a bundle on this machine
+forge profile delete <name>          # remove a user profile
+```
+
+All verbs accept `--json`.
+
+## The three starter presets
+
+| Profile | What it is for |
+| --- | --- |
+| `speed` | Pre-user speed: ship fast, review the feature not the diff, revert fast. Stage-exit gates, the plan-approval gate, the merge gate and the TDD rail are off. |
+| `strict` | Gated shipping: every stage gate on, TDD rail on, config/AGENTS/workflow files protected. |
+| `knowledge-base` | A docs repo: no `dev`/`validate` stages, citation and grounding gates on. |
+
+Presets are **data, not code** (`BUILTIN_PROFILES` in `lib/settings-profiles.js`).
+Creating a profile never requires editing Forge.
+
+## Three design rules
+
+**1. One registry.** Every profileable setting comes from
+`lib/settings-registry.js`, which **derives** its descriptors from the resolved
+runtime graph. Add a rail, gate, stage, adapter field or role to
+`lib/core/runtime-graph.js` and it is profileable on the next run with no wiring
+here. Writes go through the sparse `lib/config-writer.js` into
+`.forge/config.yaml` — the same surface `forge gate` and `forge role` write, so
+the installed hooks read one consistent signal.
+
+**2. A profile stores nothing at apply time.** `apply` writes through the
+canonical config and records no active-profile marker anywhere. `show` **derives**
+the answer by comparing resolved state to each saved profile. A hand-tuned repo
+therefore reads `custom` — honestly — instead of claiming to match a preset it no
+longer matches. `show` also names the nearest profile and the exact drift, so
+`custom` is actionable.
+
+**3. Apply is total, so flips are reversible.** A setting absent from a profile
+is reset to its package default rather than left behind. `speed` → `strict` →
+`speed` lands byte-identical to the first `speed` apply: zero residual diff, zero
+hand-edits.
+
+## Adjusting while a profile is active
+
+Toggle something (`forge gate disable gate.merge`) and the repo drops to
+`custom`. Then choose:
+
+- `forge profile save` — fold the adjustment into the profile you are closest to
+  (user profiles only; builtin presets refuse and tell you to use `--as`).
+- `forge profile save --as my-regime` — keep the original and move onto a new
+  profile.
+- do nothing — you stay on `custom`, which is the honest answer.
+
+## Where profiles live
+
+Per-user plain YAML files under `<home>/.forge/profiles/<name>.yaml`
+(`FORGE_HOME` overrides the home). A user file **shadows** a builtin of the same
+name, so presets are forkable. `export`/`import` move a regime between machines.
+
+## Not the same thing as workflow profiles
+
+`lib/workflow-profiles.js` ("workflow profiles") picks which **stage set** a user
+type runs — a different axis entirely. `forge profile` configures the settings
+surface, whatever stage set is in play. The two do not compose or conflict.
